<PLUGIN name="&name;"
        author="&author;"
        version="&version;"
        launch="&launch;"
        pluginURL="&pluginURL;"
        min="6.10.0"
        support="https://forums.unraid.net/topic/199510-support-junkerderprovinz-shiplog/">

<!-- The name is the lowercase slug, so Unraid finds the logo
     (plugins/<slug>/images/<slug>.png) and the Plugins-tab description
     (plugins/<slug>/README.md), which carries the display name. -->
<ICON>
/usr/local/emhttp/plugins/&slug;/images/&slug;.png
</ICON>

<CHANGES>
Shows the changelog of your installed Docker containers, right in the Docker tab:
what changed from your running image to the newest, with a risk badge and the
known vulnerabilities. Optional AI summaries (Ollama, llama-swap or OpenRouter), alerts on
Unraid, Discord, Matrix and more, and an auto-update that backs up first when
your fortress has a backup app.

### 3.9.8
The updates one check finds can arrive in a single message, and OpenRouter summaries fall back past a busy free model and are kept between checks.

✨ **Added**
- **The updates one check finds can arrive in a single message.** Turn on One message per check under Settings → ShipLog → Notifications → Update notices, and ShipLog sends them together, the riskiest first. Unraid's notifications show them on one line. Thanks to @repires for the idea and the work ([#150](https://github.com/junkerderprovinz/shiplog/issues/150)).
- **Lowest risk to announce holds back the message for an update below the risk you pick.** It still shows in the Docker tab, an update whose risk is unknown always goes out, and the message follows once a later check finds the update at the risk you picked.
- **A webhook or another channel that lists its events gets the grouped message as `update.batch`.** Add that event kind to it, or it keeps only the single update messages.
- **While TollRoll is your fortress's hub, the Update notices card says so.** TollRoll takes each update on its own and sends them its own way, and the lowest risk still applies.

⚡ **Improved**
- **OpenRouter falls back past a busy free model and keeps its summaries.** Several models separated by commas are tried in order, and a list of only free models ends with openrouter/free. A summary stays from one check to the next while the notes and the model stay the same, so a free model's daily allowance lasts. Thanks to @repires for the report and the fix ([#154](https://github.com/junkerderprovinz/shiplog/issues/154)).
- **Picking a model from the list adds it to the models already in the field** instead of replacing them.

🌐 **Translations**
- **The new settings and the OpenRouter hint in all 26 languages,** with clearer wording for the update notices in Romanian, Chinese, Korean, Finnish, Hungarian, Spanish, Portuguese, Russian and Ukrainian.

### 3.9.7
ShipLog starts even when its data folder is mounted a little late, and the CA Auto Update warning shows only when it updates containers too.

🐛 **Fixed**
- **ShipLog starts even when its data folder is mounted a little late.** If the folder wasn't there yet when the array finished starting, ShipLog stayed stopped until the next array start. It now waits up to a minute for the folder. Thanks to @repires for the report and the fix ([#157](https://github.com/junkerderprovinz/shiplog/issues/157)).
- **The CA Auto Update warning shows only when it updates containers too.** It appeared whenever CA Auto Update was installed, also when it only updated plugins. It now also says what to do, in all 26 languages. Thanks to @repires for pointing it out ([#152](https://github.com/junkerderprovinz/shiplog/issues/152)).

### 3.9.6
Each notification channel switches on and off in its row, and the backup before an update reaches the backup app while it reconnects.

🎨 **Design**
- **Built on GlimStone 4.9.2,** each notification channel has its own switch in its row, next to Send a test, Edit and Remove, and flipping it saves right away. A test shows how it went on its button and in the channel's line, and a channel without an address says so.
- **A switch the service refuses opens the channel's form** with the reason, so you can fix the channel there.
- **An empty list of channels shows one quiet line** over Add a channel.

⚡ **Improved**
- **ParleyPort 1.3.8.** The fortress and notification parts come from ParleyPort now. Your fortress, your notification channels and your settings carry over as they are.
- **Send a test also works on a channel that is switched off.**

🐛 **Fixed**
- **The backup before an update reaches the backup app while it reconnects, and runs only once.** If the backup app had just joined again or changed its name, the request could fail, and a request whose answer got lost could start a second backup.
- **A channel you remove comes back when the save fails.** It vanished from the page although nothing was saved.

🌐 **Translations**
- **No address yet and Switched off in all 26 languages.**

### 3.9.5
Changelog files come only from the public internet, the status page fits a phone, and the Version card waits for a real check before it calls ShipLog up to date.

🎨 **Design**
- **GlimStone 4.9.0.** Until Unraid has looked for updates once, the Version card says Not checked yet, and Check for updates is ready to click. Up to date only shows once a check is behind it.
- **Version numbers are easier to tap.** Each number on the Info tab has a 24px target. A long one, such as a test build's, moves under ShipLog's name on a phone, and the Components card does the same on a narrow screen.

⚡ **Improved**
- **The fortress and notification parts come from kit 1.1.0.** Your fortress, your notification channels and your settings carry over as they are.
- **ShipLog is an Unraid plugin only.** The Docker image for other hosts is gone, and the plugin works as before.

🐛 **Fixed**
- **Changelog files come only from the public internet.** A changelog source can no longer point ShipLog at an address in your own network or on the server itself. A source like that, saved earlier, is skipped and named once in the log.
- **The status page fits a phone.** On a narrow screen each container becomes a card with its versions, risk and changelog stacked, so nothing scrolls sideways.
- **The source field stays folded.** On the status page the field for a changelog source opens when you click **source**, instead of showing on every row.

🌐 **Translations**
- **Not checked yet in all 26 languages.**

### 3.9.4
The Info tab shows where ShipLog's updates stand, and the vulnerability chip tells the flaws with a fix from those that have none yet.

✨ **Added**
- **A Version card on the Info tab.** It shows ShipLog with its number and where its updates stand, as Unraid's Plugins tab sees them. Check for updates runs the same check as the Plugins tab, and once a new version is out, What's new opens its notes and a button takes you to the Plugins tab. The first time you open Info after a reboot, the card runs the check by itself.
- **A Components card.** GlimStone, the design system ShipLog is drawn with, has its row there, and the (i) beside its name says what it is.

🎨 **Design**
- **GlimStone 4.8.2.** The Info tab has the four cards of the other apps of the house, in their order: Version, Components, Help and About.
- **Version numbers open the newest release.** Each number on the Info tab is plain text without an underline, takes the accent's ink under the pointer and opens its project's newest release. A build that is not a release says so and links nowhere.

⚡ **Improved**
- **Vulnerabilities with a fix stand apart from those without one.** The chip on the Docker tab counts the critical and high ones a fixed version exists for and adds the rest dimmed beside them, as in "· 3 no fix yet". Only the first kind colours the chip, so a chip with nothing but unfixed ones stays a quiet grey.
- **Both numbers wherever vulnerabilities show.** The changelog window lists the ones with a fix first and tags the others "no fix yet", and the status page and the notifications give both counts. A flaw the update closes counts as one with a fix, even where the database knows no fixed version, as when the newest image drops the package.
- **One slower check after the update.** ShipLog reads the vulnerability records from osv.dev once more to learn which of them have a fix, so the first check takes a little longer.

🐛 **Fixed**
- **No underline on the Docker tab chips.** Unraid underlined the changelog and vulnerability chips, the Update all button and the buttons in the changelog window under the pointer. They keep their look.

🌐 **Translations**
- **The Version and Components cards and the new vulnerability texts in all 26 languages.**

### 3.9.3
AI summaries can run through OpenRouter on a server without a model of its own, and the settings follow GlimStone 4.8.1.

✨ **Added**
- **OpenRouter for AI summaries.** Under Settings &gt; ShipLog &gt; Sources, the AI summaries card asks for a provider: Local, which is Ollama or llama-swap as before, or OpenRouter with a model from its public list and an API key. A note next to the choice says what leaves your server with OpenRouter: the changelog text, the image name and the running and new version. Local stays the default, and nothing goes to OpenRouter while it is chosen. Thanks to @repires for the idea.
- **Two checks that answer apart.** One asks OpenRouter whether it takes the key and lists the model, without generating anything. The other runs one small real summary. Each says what went wrong: a key it refuses, no credit left, a model it does not offer right now, rate limiting or no connection.
- **The key stays on your server.** ShipLog keeps it in a file on the flash drive that only root can read, the page shows its last four characters and nothing more, and the key goes to openrouter.ai alone, never into a URL, a log line or an error. Uninstalling ShipLog deletes the key, while the other settings stay as before.

🎨 **Design**
- **Notification channels as connection rows.** Each channel is one row as in the other apps of the house: the service's logo on its tile, the name, what it sends and to which server, then Send a test, Edit and Remove. Edit opens the channel in a window, where it can also be switched off, and a channel that is off says so on its row.
- **Versions are plain text.** The versions on the Info tab no longer look like buttons. Each one is text, and the link to its release notes shows only under the pointer.
- **GlimStone 4.8.1.** The channel window keeps its buttons in one row and puts a long test result on a line of its own above them. Button labels keep the tails of Arabic letters, and a logo drawn to the edge of its tile keeps its soft edge.

🐛 **Fixed**
- **Your GitHub and Docker Hub tokens stay private.** The checks on the settings page sent them as part of a URL, which ends up in the web server's access log. They now travel in the body of a request, and the settings file that holds them is readable by root alone. Installing or updating ShipLog corrects a file that was readable by everyone.

🌐 **Translations**
- **The new texts in all 26 languages,** and the (i) of the AI card names OpenRouter next to the local models.

### 3.9.2
Names in the channel window and on the buttons in Info keep their descenders.

🐛 **Fixed**
- **Tile names keep their descenders.** The bottom of a g, p or y in a tile's name in the channel window is no longer cut off.
- **GlimStone 4.7.0.** The buttons under Help and About in Info keep their descenders too.

🌐 **Translations**
- **Hebrew uses the same word for a session as the other apps,** in the note that it ran out.

### 3.9.1
The buttons on the settings page size their labels again.

🐛 **Fixed**
- **Buttons on the settings page fit their labels again.** The channel card's buttons have labels of their own, and the script that sizes the page's button labels stopped at them. With TowerTorch's reactive or glyph-only labels, the other buttons on the page then kept their plain width and showed no tip. ShipLog leaves the card's buttons to themselves.

### 3.9.0
ShipLog names the known vulnerabilities in your running images, can hand its notifications to TollRoll, and checks a new container within a minute.

✨ **Added**
- **Known vulnerabilities of running images.** ShipLog looks up the packages of each running image in the open vulnerability database at osv.dev. A chip next to the container on the Docker tab counts the known critical and high ones, and the changelog window lists them, says when and on which image they were checked and how many the newest image closes. Only package names and versions leave your server. The check is on by default and can be switched off under Settings > ShipLog > Updates > Known vulnerabilities.
- **An update that closes a critical vulnerability moves its risk badge up a step.** The changelog window and the auto-update summary name what it closes.
- **The status page and notifications name the known vulnerabilities** of a container.
- **The window says what to do when no update closes them:** ask the project for a fixed image, or switch to another tag or image.
- **A new channel card on the Notifications tab.** You add a channel in a window with the services' tiles from GlimStone's catalog: Unraid, Discord, Slack, ntfy, Gotify, Matrix, Apprise, email over SMTP and webhooks. Each channel has its own switch, a choice of what it sends and a test that works before you save. Every change saves at once and takes effect without a restart, and the channels you had come along as they are.
- **Notifications through TollRoll.** When a TollRoll in your fortress takes over the notifications of its apps, ShipLog hands its messages to it, and the Notifications tab says so. Once a container runs the update a message offered, TollRoll hears that the message is done. If TollRoll stops taking them, ShipLog's own channels send again.
- **Discord on the Help card.** The card offers GitHub and the ShipLog Discord server in place of the forum.

🎨 **Design**
- **GlimStone 4.6.0.** Buttons, tabs and tiles light in a soft tint of your accent when the pointer reaches them, and quiet buttons keep their label readable on it. With TowerTorch they take its hover colour, the same as TowerTorch's own rows and buttons.
- **Open dropdowns come from TowerTorch.** With TowerTorch, a list on the settings page opens as plain rows with a check on the chosen option, drawn the way TowerTorch draws its own.
- **A focus ring you can see.** Keyboard focus is a solid ring in the accent's ink instead of a faint halo, 2px wide and 3px when your system asks for more contrast.
- **Placeholders you can read.** The hint in an empty field reaches 4.5:1 on every background.
- **Reactive buttons keep their width.** In TowerTorch's reactive mode the buttons in the coffee, PayPal and crypto windows and the GitHub and Update buttons in the changelog window are as wide as with their glyph and words, and the words open inside them instead of the button growing under the pointer.

⚡ **Improved**
- **Vulnerabilities count as your distribution rates them.** A distribution's own priority for a flaw comes before the raw CVSS score, and packages built from the kernel are left out, since a container runs the host's kernel.
- **The reason for a risk badge in your language, in full.** The changelog window gives it in the language of your Unraid, on a line of its own under the version and the buttons, so a longer translation is never cut short.
- **Unraid's leftover update entry, explained.** When Unraid still lists an update the container already runs, the changelog window says so in plain words and tells you to click Check for Updates at the bottom of the Docker tab.
- **Screen readers announce the changelog window as a modal dialog** and leave the page behind it alone.

🐛 **Fixed**
- **A new or recreated container gets its changelog chip right away.** A container installed from a template, or one Unraid recreates under a new ID after an update, waited for the next sweep, six hours by default, before ShipLog checked it. ShipLog checks such a container as soon as it starts, and a look once a minute catches any start it missed. Only the new container is checked, and one started with `docker run` outside Unraid waits for the next sweep. Thanks to @repires (#131, #130).
- **The changelog chip shows on containers in folders.** A folder plugin rebuilds the rows of the containers it groups, and the rebuilt rows kept ShipLog's mark but lost the chip, so those containers stayed without one. ShipLog tags such a row again (thanks @repires, #124, #123).
- **A fresh install from Community Applications goes through again.** Its first step tidied old packages in a folder a new install does not have yet, and the failed lookup stopped the install. Thanks to @Revan335 (#117).
- **golang.org/x/net 0.63.0.** It closes the 17 advisories a security scan found in ShipLog's engine, among them a denial of service in the HTML parser.
- **The fortress kit is at 1.0.5.** An app holding the phrase could announce an address that sent ShipLog's direct call to another page of a host on your network, and the call now always goes where it belongs. Two apps that first meet while one of them restarts find each other again, and the home network service starts on a host with a single network interface.

🌐 **Translations**
- **The vulnerability texts, the channel card and the TollRoll note** are there in all 26 languages.
- **The Help sentence names GitHub and Discord in all 26 languages**, and French readers are addressed with tu.

### 3.8.0
ShipLog moves into the fortress: a backup before every auto-update, and a way back when one goes sideways.

✨ **Added**
- **Every auto-update is backed up first.** When ShipLog is in a fortress with a backup app on the same server, it asks that app to back up each container before the update and waits until the backup is done (30 minutes at most, adjustable), even for a container that app has never backed up before. If the backup fails or the app doesn't answer, the update waits and ShipLog tells you why. For a container that needs no backup, turn on "Update without a backup" in its ShipLog window.
- **The backup app on your server can go by any name.** ShipLog finds it by the server it runs on, so renaming it in its own settings doesn't cost you a backup. The other apps of your fortress show ShipLog with its own logo.
- **A failed auto-update goes back to the old image.** ShipLog keeps the old image while the update runs. If the new one fails its check, turns unhealthy or stops, ShipLog points the template at the old image and recreates the container from it. Your appdata stays as it is, and the message names the backup from before the update, which waits in BombVault. Without a backup app in your fortress, auto-updates run as they always did.
- **ShipLog joins your fortress with the phrase.** The Fortress tab, just above Info, takes the twelve words. ShipLog keeps its own keys only, never the phrase. When the fortress gets a new phrase, ShipLog follows along, and if it can't, the card asks you for the new words. If another member takes ShipLog out, the card says so, after a restart too, and once the phrase has changed, the new one brings it back.
- **You can take another app out of your fortress.** The Fortress card lists the other apps, each with Remove. Once one is out, ShipLog offers to change the phrase in an app that keeps it. Until you do, your fortress takes no new app, an app that tries to join is told why, and the card names the apps you removed.
- **The fortress looks the same in every plugin.** ShipLog, TowerTorch, FireSquire and TollRoll share one fortress card, each in its own Fortress tab just above Info, and from there ShipLog can also leave the fortress and show how another plugin is doing.
- **ShipLog tells the other apps of your fortress how it is doing.** They can read how many containers have an update, which of them are a major jump or come with a breaking change, an auto-update that failed, the auto-update that is running and the auto-update log. Only members of your fortress can read it. At home they reach ShipLog directly, relay or not, on a port that lets in fortress members and nobody else. Open on ShipLog's card in another app shows all of this in a window, since ShipLog's own pages stay behind your Unraid login.
- **Other apps of your fortress can have ShipLog update containers.** A maintenance plan names the containers it wants, or none for every container with an update, and ShipLog updates them one at a time with the same backup and rollback as its own auto-update. It never runs two updates at once. A backup app can also ask which containers ShipLog's auto-update looks after, so the two of them never update the same one.
- **Each release gets its own card in the changelog window.** The card is headed by the release's version, with its date beside it and a link to its page in the corner.
- **Longer AI summaries that cover the whole jump.** When an update skips releases, the summary reads every one of them, not just the newest, and lists up to ten changes, each with a sentence on what it means for the container. A short changelog still gets a short summary. A container on a rolling tag such as `latest` is summarised from its newest release, since its recent releases can belong to other channels.

🎨 **Design**
- **GlimStone 4.4.1.** Update now and Update all show the two arrows of the sync glyph. With TowerTorch, the Repository, Close and release links take the accent when the pointer reaches them, and under its rainbow each one takes a colour of its own. The buttons in the coffee, PayPal and crypto windows do the same. A held or rolled back update shows in orange, the colour of a warning, and the badges in the changelog window keep readable text on every accent.
- **Ready for OLED screens.** When TowerTorch puts its dark look on true black, the changelog window and the coffee, PayPal and crypto windows step down with it.
- **The versions at the top of the changelog window are larger,** with the newest version in bold.
- **The auto-update switch in the changelog window only shows while the scheduled auto-update is on.**

⚡ **Improved**
- **Saving says so.** A switch in the changelog window shows "Saved" for a moment. With TowerTorch, a setting that couldn't be saved also tells you why in a toast, on the settings page and in the changelog window.
- **Fewer, more useful notifications.** An update that auto-update will apply anyway now appears only in its run summary, so no notice is left behind for an update that is already installed. Critical updates and updates auto-update will not apply still notify you straight away.
- **Notifications have a file of their own.** On its first start, ShipLog moves your Matrix, Apprise and Unraid settings from shiplog.cfg to notify.json next to it, and the settings page saves them there from then on. A channel that was only partly filled in comes over switched off, so nothing you typed is lost.
- **A message that does not get through is tried three times** instead of once. ShipLog no longer follows a redirect to another host, because the request can carry your token.
- **Unraid shows a failed update as a warning.** That goes for an auto-update run in which an update failed and for an update that did not apply. Apprise messages now carry a title.
- **`latest` follows releases.** The container image's `latest` tag is always the newest release. The tip of development is published as `edge`, and each release also gets version tags.
- **Made for TowerTorch 1.4.1 and newer.** ShipLog follows TowerTorch's new internal names, so please update both together to keep the shared look.

🐛 **Fixed**
- **Only the Unraid web UI reaches ShipLog's engine.** The engine answered on port 8484 to anyone on your network, without a login, so any device there could start a check or change a changelog source. It now listens on a local socket, and the Docker tab, the settings page and the status page reach it through your Unraid login. The status page opens from ShipLog's settings page, and the port setting is gone.
- **Auto-update keeps your containers.** If a container is missing after Unraid's update script has run, or disappears a few minutes later, ShipLog recreates it from its template. A container that was running before is started again, and a stopped one stays stopped.
- **The update script's output goes to the log** whenever an update does not go through cleanly, so a failed update can be traced.
- **Built with Go 1.27.4.** Release builds had used the older Go version named in go.mod. The new one also brings Go's fix for a crash in its web server.
- **No more false critical alerts on a rebuild.** An image rebuilt without a new release could turn critical because of a word in older release notes. When the running version is unknown, ShipLog now reads only the notes of a version the image is proven to be, so a real breaking release still alerts you.
- **Notifications name only a version they can vouch for.** A rebuilt image reads "New image for latest" instead of naming a release it does not contain. The changelog window's header follows the same rule.
- **No unproven version on an up-to-date container.** A rolling tag whose version is unknown shows the tag, not the newest release.
- **ShipLog comes back after a reboot without network.** Installing ShipLog removed the very package it was about to use from the flash. Unraid installs every plugin again at boot, often before the network is up, so a start without network came up without ShipLog.
- **The settings page saves again.** A name clash in the crypto window kept the settings page from saving anything once it had loaded, and joining the fortress stood still with its button locked.
- **The name in the banner reads on a phone.** Below 600 px it runs across the watermark instead of shrinking to two letters.
- **The AI model chip reads again with TowerTorch.** Beside Summary it was grey on the accent colour. It takes the badge's text colour.

🌐 **Translations**
- **The notification help and the new fortress, backup and rollback texts** are there in all 26 languages.
- **The same words as the other apps.** Fortress, phrase, member and backup read the same in ShipLog as in the rest of your fortress, in every language.

### 3.7.0
✨ **Added**
- **Apprise as a notification channel.** On the Notifications tab, enter the address of an Apprise API server or an Apprise URL and send a test message. ShipLog then sends the same messages it sends to Matrix, which reaches more than 100 services.
- **Coffee, PayPal and crypto windows in the page.** The giving buttons on the Info tab open a window inside the settings page instead of a new tab. Each coin's logo shows in its own colour, and Close and Copy show glyphs that follow TowerTorch's label mode.

🎨 **Design**
- **A new settings page** on GlimStone 3.8.0, laid out like TowerTorch's: tabs on the left, cards with a title badge, the label on the left and the control on the right edge. A banner with the logo and the claim heads the page, and the Info tab is split into Version, Help and About cards. The Version card names the GlimStone version and shows version numbers without the leading `v`.
- **Settings save as you change them.** Done and Apply are gone. A switch saves at once and springs back if saving fails, a text field saves shortly after you stop typing, and the engine picks up the change without a restart.
- **Glyphs on the settings page.** The Updates tab, the Check buttons, Send test message on the Notifications tab and Autodetect on the Sources tab show glyphs.
- **The changelog window on GlimStone 3.8.0, with glyphs.** Update now, Update all, Close, the Repository and changelog links and the warnings show a glyph where they had a text arrow or symbol. With TowerTorch, the buttons follow its label mode, and the pulse on a critical update, the scrolling line and the fade behind the window stop when TowerTorch's Motion is set to Off.
- **A new logo**, an anchor in a silver ring that reads on light and dark themes. It shows on the settings page, in the Unraid menu and in the Community Applications icon.

🐛 **Fixed**
- A tag with a `v` or `release-` prefix counts as the same version as one without, so ShipLog no longer reports an update to the release you already run.
- A version label a container inherits from its Ubuntu base image no longer passes for the app's version.
- When Unraid still lists an update that ShipLog's last check already saw installed, the changelog window says so instead of showing a stale verdict. For an image without a source repository, it explains where to set one.
- A quote in a CA deprecation note can no longer break out of the changelog window's tooltip.
- With Unraid's white theme and TowerTorch, the changelog window in the Docker tab kept its near-black frame, so the dark header text was unreadable. The window stays light in a light theme.
- With TowerTorch, the changelog window and the settings page took on its look only while TowerTorch's header area was switched on, which it is not by default. Both follow TowerTorch whenever its theming is on, and the settings page has the look from the start.

🌐 **Translations**
- "Repository" in the changelog window is translated into all 26 languages.

### 3.6.0
Most of this release started in @nphil's fork (https://github.com/nphil/shiplog), tests included. Thank you!

✨ **Added**
- You can leave single containers out of the scheduled auto-update: switch them off under Settings, Updates, Containers, or with the switch in a container's changelog window. An excluded container shows an "Auto-update off" pill next to its chip, and the auto-update log lists it as skipped.
- Changelog summaries can come from llama-swap or any other OpenAI-compatible server, such as LM Studio or vLLM, with a Check button in the settings. Without a llama-swap URL, Ollama works as before.
- Private images resolve with the registry logins in Docker's config.json, the same logins Unraid's own update check uses.
- A container pinned to a version tag gets a blue hint when a newer version exists. Pulling the pinned tag again cannot reach that version, so the container shows no update and has no auto-update switch, and the hint tells you to change the tag.

⚡ **Improved**
- Auto-update leaves a container alone when it already runs the newest image.
- When a dry run only skipped excluded containers, the log says it was a dry run.

🎨 **Design**
- The README is shorter and shows the changelog window and the status page in new pictures, with buttons to install ShipLog from Community Applications or download the source.

🐛 **Fixed**
- No more phantom updates. Whether a container has an update depends only on the image behind its own tag. ShipLog used to compare against the highest version tag in the registry, which was often an old date tag, a beta channel or a version label inherited from the base image.
- An update could count as done while the container kept running the old image. Unraid's update script ends without an error even when the pull fails or the container is not recreated, so auto-update reported the same container as updated night after night. Auto-update now checks the running image afterwards and reports a miss as failed.
- The same check runs after an update started from the Docker tab. Once Unraid's update window is done, ShipLog asks whether the container runs the new image: if it doesn't, you get a notification and the row keeps its update badge, and if it does, the badge switches to up to date right away.
- A container whose newer image was already pulled but not applied lost its update badge, because Docker then lists it by image ID only. ShipLog now reads the image name from the container's own settings.
- Your own apps no longer show "Removed from Community Applications". Only apps from repositories that Community Applications crawls get its verdicts. An app installed from an old copy of a template repository that has since moved is looked up by its image, and it counts as removed only when CA no longer lists that image.
- The Docker Hub username and token from the settings never reached the engine, so Docker Hub images stayed on the anonymous rate limit.
- After a quick restart, the engine could exit because the previous instance still held the database. It now waits for the lock.
- The "Auto-update off" pill no longer sits indented when it wraps under the chip.
- The switch in the changelog window counts a change as saved only after ShipLog has restarted with it. Before, any answer from Unraid's settings handler counted.

🌐 **Translations**
- The new texts in the settings and the Docker tab are translated into all 26 languages.

### 3.5.0
✨ **Added**
- A changelog file can be the changelog source. Some projects never publish GitHub releases and keep a CHANGELOG.md or History.txt instead, Domoticz's beta channel for one. Click **source** on the status page and paste the file's URL; a link to GitHub's file view works too. If the file has version headings, the bubble shows the sections between your running and the newest version, or the newest section when ShipLog can't place your version. A file without version headings shows its first 40 lines. Thanks to reinv for the idea.
- A version line at the bottom of the settings page (ShipLog vX.Y.Z · GlimStone vX.Y.Z), as in the other GlimStone apps.

### 3.4.0
✨ **Added**
- A "mute unmaintained" toggle on the status page, next to the existing "source" override. Silences the Unmaintained badge for that container permanently, covering every detection trigger at once - registry-gone, archived-source-repo, and both Community Applications signals - including the two the automatic exemptions below don't reach. Works identically for self-built/template-less containers as for Community-Applications-installed ones, since it's keyed by the image repo, not by whether a template exists.

⚡ **Improved**
- The Docker-tab watcher that keeps container rows tagged now coalesces bursts of page updates into one pass instead of reacting to every single one. A streaming container-update log can mutate the page hundreds of times per second; on a host with many containers this used to add unnecessary work on top of an already busy tab during an update.

🐛 **Fixed**
- A self-built app (a hand-authored template, or a plain docker run with no template at all) could get permanently branded "Discontinued" - the two-crawl Community Applications absence check treated its natural absence from the CA feed as removal, even though it was never listed there to begin with. Now skipped entirely for any container without a CA TemplateURL.
- A changelog-source override (telling ShipLog where YOUR app's changelog actually lives) now also exempts that app from the CA verdicts. A template pointing into your own private repo 404s publicly, which used to trip the same false "removed from Community Applications" verdict on top of the override already in place. Credit: nphil, who found and fixed both of these independently.
- (with TowerTorch installed) The topmost section of the changelog bubble, often the AI summary, could lose its card background and blend into the window instead, and a Discontinued container's header could still show a stray drop shadow. Every section now consistently keeps its card styling regardless of position.

### 3.3.0
🎨 **Design**
- "Not maintained" is renamed to "Discontinued" throughout (all languages) — the old wording was easy to confuse with the separate "editorially deprecated" state.
- The "editorially deprecated in Community Applications" state now replaces the Docker-tab changelog chip with its own amber button, the same way the red "Discontinued" button already does for a real dead end. Its own amber pill and explanatory note appear in the changelog bubble too, mirroring "Discontinued" there as well. Still its own colour and wording throughout: the app is still listed and still genuinely receiving updates, so it reads as a lesser warning, not the same severity as a real dead end.
- **(with TowerTorch installed)** The "Removed from Community Applications" / "editorially deprecated" note now renders as its own title-badge card, matching the changelog window's other sections. Only the very first section under the header blends into the window background; every section below it keeps its own card look.
- **(with TowerTorch installed)** Section cards in the changelog window no longer sit close enough for a card's title badge to overlap the card above it.

🐛 **Fixed**
- **Containers renamed away from their Community Applications template name, or running as a suffixed multi-instance copy, were wrongly flagged "removed from Community Applications."** The catalog lookup matched only your container's own display name against the feed — a container named differently than its template (renaming is common, and so is running several instances of one template with a suffix) could never match, and after two crawls without a name hit was reported as confirmed absent even though it was genuinely still listed. Lookups now also try the container's repository and installed template URL before concluding it's gone. Also fixed: a stale, no-longer-valid template URL could win over a catalog lookup that positively confirms the app is still listed under its current location.
- The header's "Update now" and "Repository" buttons could shift position from container to container; they now always stay inline, top-right of the header, on every container.
- A critical-risk badge showed both the plain risk dot and its warning triangle at once; the redundant dot is gone.
- The risk-reason text next to a badge is now a real scrolling marquee when it's too long to fit, instead of being cut short with an ellipsis.
- **(with TowerTorch installed)** The "Discontinued" and "Editorially deprecated" badges/buttons had washed out to a barely visible tint instead of their intended solid red/amber.
- The changelog window (with TowerTorch installed) now matches TowerTorch's own Docker update window exactly: the window background is the correct darker shade instead of a lighter generic surface, a stray third background layer inside the section cards is gone, and the section title badges now sit half on the card's own top edge like a real form legend instead of flush inside the card.
- **(with TowerTorch installed)** The topmost section's background no longer casts a stray drop shadow now that it matches the window behind it.

### 3.2.0
✨ **Added**
- **Real Community Applications catalog corroboration for the "Not maintained" warning.** ShipLog now fetches the same feed CA's own plugin reads, cached and refreshed only when it actually changes (no extra load on your GitHub rate limit), instead of relying only on whether an app's historical template file still happens to be reachable at its old raw GitHub URL. That raw-URL check can never see an app that was pulled from Community Applications while its template file happens to still sit untouched in its source repo, or one a moderator blacklisted directly — both are now caught. Same fail-open discipline as the raw-URL check: an ambiguous match (two apps sharing a display name) or a feed fetch failure never flags anything, and an apparent removal only counts once it holds across two separate crawls of the feed, since the feed itself is crowd-sourced and can have a transient gap for a renamed listing.
- **New "editorially deprecated in CA" signal, separate from "Not maintained".** Community Applications also has a softer state: an app hidden from default search with a moderator note pointing at a better-maintained alternative, while still installable and still genuinely receiving updates (the real case that prompted this: HandBrake's `coppit/handbrake` listing, deprecated in favour of `jlesage/handbrake`, still updates fine). Unlike "Not maintained" this never replaces the changelog view — it is exposed as new `ca_deprecated` / `ca_deprecated_note` fields on the status API for now; a visual badge on the Docker-tab chip is a follow-up.

🎨 **Design**
- **The TowerTorch-integrated look is fully border-free again and matches its own locked recipe.** Several elements inside the changelog window and the update-window card (section dividers, the GitHub/Update buttons, the risk/DIGEST pills, the AI-summary badge) had drifted back to literal border lines in follow-up feature work, contradicting GlimStone's shade-only convention; all of them now use the same box-shadow-based shading as the rest of TowerTorch, gated behind TowerTorch actually being installed so the plain native-Unraid look is untouched without it. Card and button colours now also match TowerTorch's own locked update-window recipe (a dark frame holding explicit light cards) instead of a token-driven shade too close to the frame to read as separate.
- Section headings on the changelog window's cards ("Summary", "Recent releases") are now a filled accent badge, matching TowerTorch's own update-window recipe, instead of a full-width text bar.

🐛 **Fixed**
- **The "Not maintained" warning could re-notify on every single sweep instead of once.** Its dedupe check reads the previous sweep's row back from the database, but the two columns behind it were never actually written there — a leftover gap from when the feature first shipped. An app that stayed unmaintained for days could re-trigger a Matrix / Unraid notification every 6 hours (the default poll interval) for as long as it stayed flagged, instead of exactly once on the transition into the state.
- **The "Not maintained" warning could still fire on apps that are perfectly fine**, this time through its own corroboration check: a GitHub rate limit, a timeout, or any error probing the source repository was treated as confirmation the repo is gone — the same fail-closed mistake the 3.1.0 fix for this feature was meant to close, one layer deeper. Only a clean 404/410 on the repo page now counts as confirmation; anything inconclusive leaves the app alone.

### 3.1.0
✨ **Added**
- **"Not maintained" warning for dead-end apps.** ShipLog now flags an installed app that has reached a dead end, on a separate axis from the update risk: its Unraid template was pulled from Community Applications (its source template now 404s), its image was deleted from the registry, or its upstream source repository was archived. In the Docker tab a static red "Not maintained" button replaces the Changelog chip (click it for the exact reason, plus the last changelog if one is still available), and a single Matrix / Unraid notification fires when an app first turns unmaintained. Healthy apps are untouched. This closes the gap that let an installed app quietly stop getting updates without you noticing (the case that prompted it: an app removed from CA).

🐛 **Fixed**
- The "critical" breaking-change badge no longer fires on updates that carry no new version. A same-tag digest rebuild (":latest" moving to a fresh build of the SAME version), and release notes belonging to the version you already run, were still scanned for breaking-change wording  -  so a harmless rebuild could show a red "critical" badge. Immich was flagged for its own current release's note, and Redis for a "data loss" line in a version installed long ago. ShipLog now only scans release notes for versions strictly newer than the running one, and skips the raw-notes fallback entirely when the update does not advance the version. Genuine breaking changes in a real update still escalate to critical.
- **The "Not maintained" warning no longer fires on apps that are still perfectly fine in Community Applications.** It checked only the app's historical template URL for a 404, which is far too fragile a signal on its own: that link also goes dead when its source repo gets renamed (raw GitHub links don't follow a rename, unlike the repo page itself) or its template file simply moves to a new path, both routine maintenance and nothing to do with removal. Seen firing on several genuinely active apps in the same scan. ShipLog now double-checks a dead raw-GitHub link against the source repository itself before calling an app unmaintained.

🌐 **Translations**
- Translated the new "Not maintained" warning (the button label and its reason hint) into all 26 supported languages.

### 3.0.0
✨ **Added**
- New "critical" risk level for breaking updates. When an upstream release note flags a breaking change (a required database migration, a removed extension, a dropped API), ShipLog now escalates that update to critical and shows a red badge, instead of grading a rolling ":latest" digest move as a harmless "low". This is the case that lets a breaking swap (for example Immich moving off pgvecto.rs to VectorChord) slip past unnoticed.
- New "Native Unraid notifications" setting (community request, off by default), under Settings -> Notifications. Turn it on and ShipLog also sends its update alerts through Unraid's own notification system: they show in the notification centre and fan out to every agent you configured (email, Discord, Telegram, ...), alongside any Matrix alerts. A major update comes through as a "warning", the rest as "normal". Translated across the 26 supported languages.
- New "Ignore third-party containers" setting (community request, off by default), under Settings -> General. Turn it on and ShipLog tracks only containers created from an Unraid template, skipping anything started by Docker Compose, Dockhand or a plain docker run (containers without Unraid's net.unraid.docker.managed label)  -  the same third-party distinction CA Auto Update uses. They drop out of the list and are no longer checked against the registry. Translated across the 26 supported languages.
- New auto-update safety switch (community suggestion): "Never if changelog mentions"  -  a comma-separated word list (e.g. "breaking, migration required"). An update that is otherwise eligible under your level policy is blocked, never applied, when the pending version's own release notes contain one of these words, case-insensitive. Catches an app that calls out a breaking change even in a minor or patch release, which the SemVer level alone can't. Blocked updates are still reported in the run summary (log + Matrix)  -  including in Dry run, so you can see the safety switch working before you trust it live. Off by default; empty word list = no effect.

🎨 **Design**
- The settings page now looks native to Unraid by default and only becomes the GlimStone look when TowerTorch is installed. Without TowerTorch you get Unraid's own form styling (native font, inputs, buttons, checkboxes and section titles); with it the page turns into cards, wells, pill tabs and toggles in your accent colour.
- Token and password fields now carry a reveal eye inside the field on the right, are as wide as every other field, and their check button sits on the line below.
- The silent-update spinner is now much more visible  -  a translucent WHITE veil over the container's logo (with a higher-contrast ring) instead of the earlier faint dark tint.

⚡ **Improved**
- The changelog for a version jump now spans every release between your running version and the newest one, newest first, so a multi-version update surfaces the intermediate release that carries the breaking note, not just the single newest release.
- A critical update raises an Unraid "alert" notification (the level above a major-version "warning"), so it stands out in the notification centre and in whatever notification agents you have wired up.
- More containers now show a real changelog. A container running a bare image-ID, a locally built image, or a digest-pinned image used to show no release notes even when the image names its source repo; ShipLog now reads that source label and shows the running version's notes anyway. Fixes the common case of your own locally rebuilt containers showing nothing.
- Curated more common images to their GitHub source, so they show real release notes instead of a bare version delta: Redis, MariaDB, Ollama, MinIO, jlesage FileBot and HandBrake, Storj, Nginx Proxy Manager, MySpeed and ClamAV.
- Changelogs now load far more reliably. ShipLog fetches each repo's release list once and caches it with an ETag, so a scan over many containers barely touches GitHub's API. The usual reason a changelog came back empty was GitHub's anonymous limit of 60 requests per hour (shared per IP) running out partway through a scan.
- Rolling images (the "latest" tag) and digest-only updates now show the repo's five most recent releases, each with its tag and date, instead of a single entry.
- When GitHub's rate limit really is reached, the bubble now says so and points you to the GitHub token field in ShipLog's settings. A personal access token (no scopes needed) raises the limit to about 5000 requests per hour.
- The container template's Project page joins the changelog-source chain (community suggestion): manual override, then the curated default, then the template's GitHub Project link, then the image's OCI source label. This fixes images whose label points at a base image (e.g. an ffmpeg wrapper) and fills in sources for images that carry no label at all.
- Uninstalling keeps the version history (/mnt/user/appdata/shiplog) instead of deleting it: a read-only advisor shouldn't remove a user-visible folder, and a reinstall now resumes with full history. The removal message says where it lives if you want it gone.
- Cleaner settings: every field's help text now hides behind a small info icon next to its label (hover or focus to read it), the same convention as the other plugins. The GitHub / Docker Hub token help keeps its links, and if scripting is off the text stays visible.
- The changelog-source override now verifies the repo when you save it: a typo or a non-existent owner/repo is caught right away (a rate-limited check never blocks the save).
- A "version delta only" row is now a clickable jump to the source repo's releases page (it used to build a version compare link that often 404s), so a manual source override is one click from the real release notes.
- Override the changelog source per image, right on the status page (IP:8484). When an image's OCI source label points at the packaging wrapper (LinuxServer's docker-app repo), is wrong (inherited from a base image), or is missing, click "source" on that row and point it at the correct GitHub repo. The engine then mines the changelog and the link from there for every container of that image.
- Common LinuxServer apps now resolve to their upstream project out of the box (Radarr, Sonarr, Lidarr, Prowlarr, Readarr, Whisparr, Bazarr) instead of the wrapper's dependency-bump log. A manual override always wins.

🐛 **Fixed**
- Fix: the settings page no longer forces its own font; it inherits Unraid's, so the text matches the rest of the WebGUI.
- Fix: the changelog window no longer renders as a rounded lozenge when TowerTorch's shape is set to pill. The window read the shape token uncapped; it is now capped so a large window keeps tight corners (rounded, square, or a max of 14px at pill), matching the other TowerTorch popups.
- Housekeeping: ShipLog now drops the leftover row for a container that was recreated with a new ID (a local rebuild, or an update that replaces the container) or removed entirely, so the list no longer accumulates stale duplicate entries for things that are gone.
- The changelog popup no longer grew by a couple of pixels every time you opened it. Its remembered size is now applied exactly, so the window stays the size you left it at instead of creeping larger with each click.
- The daemon no longer prints "shiplog: started (pid, port)" into Unraid's footer status bar on array start. The start line now goes to the ShipLog log only; genuine errors still surface. Purely cosmetic  -  nothing about the service changes.
- Changelogs render as formatted text now (headings, bold, code, links, lists), and stray HTML entities from upstream release notes no longer show literally. Everything is escaped before rendering, so no upstream HTML runs in the page.

🌐 **Translations**
- Translated the two new messages into all 26 supported languages.

### 2.0.0
✨ **Added**
- NEW: scheduled auto-update, gated by version level (Unraid plugin only, off by default). ShipLog
  can now apply container updates for you on a schedule  -  but only up to the level you choose:
  Patch, Minor or Major. Unknown / non-versioned tags are never auto-updated, and rolling ":latest"
  digest moves have their own separate toggle. Run it daily at a set time, once at boot, or every N
  hours / days. A Dry-run mode only reports what WOULD update (to the ShipLog log, plus Matrix if set
  up), so you can build trust before arming real updates. Updates are handed to Unraid's own
  container-update path (pull plus recreate
  from the container's template), so a container comes back identical to a manual update  -  the
  engine itself still never writes to the Docker socket.
- NEW: the settings page is organised into tabs (General, Updates, Notifications, Sources) so it
  stays tidy as it grows.
- NEW: a warning appears when CA Auto Update is also installed, so you do not accidentally arm both
  and double-update your containers.
- NEW: with "Silent update" on, a spinner overlays the container's logo with a white veil while the
  update runs, so you can clearly see it is in progress even though the download-log window stays
  hidden. It clears itself the moment the update finishes.
- New "Updates" settings for what happens when you click "Update now" in a changelog
  bubble (ShipLog stays read-only  -  it triggers Unraid's own update either way):
  "Ask before updating" (on by default) shows a confirmation first; "Silent update"
  (off by default) runs the update without Unraid's pop-up download-log window  -  the
  update still runs to completion in the background.
- New logo: a gold anchor in a ring, in two variants  -  a light (white) ring for the
  Plugins tile, menu icon and settings header (it pops on the dark themes and its dark
  outline keeps it readable on the light ones), and a dark ring for the README / store.
  The gold anchor is the constant, always-visible core.
- NEW: registry friendliness  -  bearer tokens are cached until expiry, duplicate
  images share one lookup per sweep, and a rate-limiting registry (including its
  token endpoint) is backed off host-wide (1m..1h) instead of hammered; prior
  verdicts carry forward with their original timestamp, so nothing flickers and
  stale data is never presented as freshly checked.
- NEW: "Update all (N)" button next to the Basic/Advanced view toggle. One click
  triggers Unraid's own bulk update for every container with a pending update.
  ShipLog stays read-only: it only relocates the native trigger. The button shows
  the pending-update count and hides itself when nothing is pending.

🎨 **Design**
- Refreshed logo: the plugin tile, menu icon and settings header now use a flip-ready
  double-ring mark that reads on every Unraid theme; the web-UI status page, the README
  banner and the store icon follow the new gold-anchor design.
- The plugin author shows as "Junker der Provinz", and the install/uninstall ceremony
  prints the house ASCII banner ("ShipLog ist ready!" after an install, "Bye!" on removal).
- Reworked ShipLog logo on the engine status page, recoloured to read on the
  dark background.
- Buttons match Unraid's native style and hover via the theme; the bubble link is
  now labelled "Repository" since a project may not live on GitHub.
- The status page shows the real ShipLog logo instead of the anchor glyph.
- The ShipLog logo now shows in the GUI, not just the Plugins tab: the Settings
  menu icon is the logo (Icon="shiplog.png") and the settings page header shows
  the logo instead of the anchor emoji. The logo already ships under icons/ +
  images/ (the FanCtrlPlus convention).
- Logo + name + description in the Plugins tab now all work, the FanCtrlPlus way:
  the package ships a README.md (shows "ShipLog" + the description) and the logo
  under images/, and the plugin slug is lowercase so Unraid resolves both. The
  v1.0.8 capital-name version showed the puzzle piece + no description.
- Attempted to keep a capital name via the ICON element (didn't resolve the logo
   -  superseded by 1.0.9).
- Show the new white-framed logo in the Plugins tab (shipped under images/ +
  icons/). (Superseded by 1.0.8  -  this version lowercased the name as a
  workaround, which broke the name + description.)
- Restores the "ShipLog" display name and the PNG logo icon.
- Plugin icon is the ShipLog logo (PNG).

⚡ **Improved**
- ShipLog grows from a read-only advisor into one that can also ACT: it can now update your
  containers for you, on a schedule, gated by version level  -  so this is a major release.
- Uninstall now also removes the engine's appdata (/mnt/user/appdata/shiplog  -  the regenerable
  version-memory) for a clean slate; your flash settings (.cfg) are still kept.
- The "Ask before updating" and "Silent update" options now also apply to Unraid's OWN
  native update controls on the Docker rows, not just the "Update now" button in ShipLog's
  changelog bubble. With "Ask before updating" off, clicking a native "apply update" runs
  without the confirmation prompt; with "Silent update" on, it runs without the pop-up
  download-log window. Scoped strictly to the per-container update controls, so other
  confirmation dialogs (e.g. removing a container) are never auto-confirmed. ShipLog stays
  read-only  -  it only automates Unraid's own dialogs on top of Unraid's own click, and if
  those dialogs ever look different on your build the update still runs, just visibly.
- Localised: the settings page and the changelog bubble now follow Unraid's configured
  language across 26 languages, from the plugin's own locale files, instead of English only.
- The two Update toggles use Unraid's orange accent and each sits on its own tidy row with
  the description on the line beneath.
- Settings redesign to a flat, borderless Carbon look: no dark backing sheet, a clean
  header, and borderless cards set apart by a lighter fill and a soft shadow.
- The engine status page and the Docker-tab chip render the new pinned/local states
  neutrally (grey) instead of a green "up to date".
- Packaging fix (thanks @jesposito): the plugin package now forces root:root
  ownership on every file. Without it a Windows-built package baked in the
  builder's uid, which upgradepkg applied to "/" on install and broke SSH key
  auth ("bad ownership or modes for directory /").
- The risk dot is now a meaningful traffic light. An up-to-date container shows
  GREEN (it was a dead grey), so a healthy box reads as a wall of green instead of
  grey; grey now means only "could not resolve a version" (e.g. an anonymous rate
  limit  -  set a GitHub token in Settings to grade it). Orange is a minor update,
  red a major one.
- Fixes the risk dot colour. A rolling ":latest" update is now graded by its real
  version jump, so a minor shows orange and a major shows red, instead of always a
  flat grey/green "digest" dot. Containers ShipLog cannot resolve a version for
  stay grey; a same-version image rebuild stays a low (green) digest update.
- The Update and Repository buttons in the changelog bubble use the Unraid accent:
  an orange outline that fills on hover.
- Shows the changelog of the pending update itself (the target release's notes)
  instead of assembling the whole version span  -  fewer registry/GitHub calls, so
  it shows something more often.
- Rate-limit (HTTP 429) robustness for registries like ghcr.io / lscr.io: honours
  Retry-After with backoff, throttles per host, reuses your GitHub token to
  authenticate those registries, and keeps the last-known result instead of
  "unknown" on a transient 429. Set a GitHub token (Settings) to mostly avoid 429
  and empty changelogs.
- "Update now" button in the changelog bubble when an update is available. ShipLog
  stays read-only: the button triggers Unraid's own apply-update for that
  container; it never writes to the Docker socket itself.
- Ollama Autodetect now finds an Ollama running as a container (it asks the Docker
  socket and probes the container's address), not just host ports.
- Shows the INSTALLED version instead of just "latest". ShipLog now reads the
  version an image declares about itself (the org.opencontainers.image.version
  label) and shows it immediately, so a rolling ":latest" container displays its
  real version on the first sweep, without waiting to witness an update. The
  digest-proven version memory still takes precedence.
- Works with Folder View 3. The Docker-tab changelog chip now finds containers
  that Folder View groups into folders; the plain Docker tab is unchanged.
- Settings: a Check button for the GitHub token (it shows your hourly rate limit,
  since an anonymous 60/h is why changelogs often come back empty; a token raises
  it to about 5000/h), an Autodetect button for Ollama (probes the common local
  ports and fills in the URL and model), and a same-origin engine-status line that
  replaces the status-page link which broke when the GUI is opened over the
  myunraid.net hostname.
- The Enable toggle is green when on, and the Apply button is a clearer primary.
- Changelog resolution now logs once when the GitHub API rate limit is hit
  (naming the cause and the fix) and skips a redundant API call for repos with no
  releases, to stretch the anonymous budget.
- Ollama: log the exact reason a summary comes back empty (HTTP status, an empty
  model response, or output that is not the expected JSON) instead of a silent
  "returned nothing", so AI-summary problems can be diagnosed.
- The Engine "Enable" setting is now an on/off toggle (a switch) instead of a
  Yes/No dropdown, and its On/Off label follows the active Unraid language
  (e.g. Ein/Aus in German), with an English fallback.
- The settings page (and the Docker bubble) actually appear now. The package
  shipped its .page files with CRLF line endings (Windows build), and Unraid's
  PageBuilder splits a page header from its body on a pure-LF "---" separator, so
  a CRLF .page fails to parse and is silently dropped (no menu entry, no bubble).
  All plugin files now ship with LF, enforced by a .gitattributes and an LF
  normalise step in the packager. The v1.0.10 page rename alone did not fix this.
- Settings page is back. It is now plugins/shiplog/shiplog.page with
  launch="Settings/shiplog" (lowercase, matching the slug), so Unraid lists it
  under Settings again. v1.0.9 lowercased the plugin name but kept the page as
  ShipLog.page + launch Settings/ShipLog, so the settings entry vanished.
- Settings: a "Show" toggle to reveal each token field, and a live "Check"
  button for the Docker Hub credentials (like the Ollama/Matrix checks).
- Optional Docker Hub credentials (Settings) raise the Docker Hub rate limit, so
  docker.io images resolve a version + changelog instead of showing an update
  with no version number.
- Settings now explain what the GitHub and Docker Hub tokens are for.
- Remembers the running version per container. A rolling ":latest" image is
  pinned to a real version once its digest matches the newest release, then
  carried forward  -  so a later update shows a true "1.7 -> 1.8" instead of
  "latest". The newest version is proven by digest, so a :latest that lags a
  newer published tag is never mislabeled.
- The bubble header now shows the remembered version as the current one.
- Resolve changelogs for ghcr.io images even without an OCI source label
  (ghcr.io/owner/repo → github.com/owner/repo), so more containers get a
  changelog + version instead of an empty "latest".
- Installer now force-reinstalls the package (upgradepkg --reinstall), fixing
  the "newer version already installed" skip when coming from the old
  date-versioned test builds.
- For an up-to-date ":latest" container, the header now shows the resolved
  version number (e.g. "1.8.0") instead of "latest".
- Release date in the bubble is now formatted DD.MM.YYYY.

🐛 **Fixed**
- FIX: with "Ask before updating" off and/or "Silent update" on, updating a container did not
  work  -  the confirmation could hang the page and the update never started. Root cause: the old
  code tried to auto-CLICK Unraid's confirmation dialog, which raced (and hid the backdrop before
  the dialog even existed), leaving the page stuck while the update never ran. That whole
  mechanism is removed. Now, with the confirm OFF, ShipLog runs Unraid's OWN update directly (the
  exact call Unraid makes when you click "Yes, update it!")  -  there is no dialog to race. With the
  confirm ON, Unraid's native prompt is shown untouched and you click it yourself. "Silent" hides
  only the download-log window and cleanly closes it the moment the update finishes. The update
  now ALWAYS runs and the page can no longer freeze  -  on every option combination, for both
  ShipLog's changelog button and Unraid's own update buttons, and repeatedly.
- FIX: with "Ask before updating" off and/or "Silent update" on, container updates stopped
  working after the first one  -  the native update buttons AND the changelog-bubble button did
  nothing. Unraid's SweetAlert reuses ONE dialog node for every popup on the page; ShipLog was
  leaving hidden/confirmed state stuck on that shared node, so every later update inherited a
  dead, invisible dialog (all containers, until a page reload). The automation is rewritten to
  leave NO state on Unraid's dialog node: it hides only the live download-log window (via a
  scoped class that can never match the confirmation dialog), and auto-confirms only a single,
  ready proceed button  -  on any unexpected markup it does nothing and leaves the dialog to you.
  A native update can no longer be blocked or cancelled by ShipLog.
- FIX: ShipLog's "update available / up to date" verdict could disagree with Unraid's own
  (e.g. a LinuxServer -lsNNN rebuild that Unraid flags but ShipLog's version compare missed,
  or a pinned tag where a newer version exists but your tag is current). The verdict now
  FOLLOWS Unraid's own live per-container status  -  so ShipLog can never contradict what the
  Docker tab shows. ShipLog's version, changelog and risk are shown as the detail on top.
- FIX: "Update now" in the changelog bubble did nothing when set to silent + no confirm
  (a follow-up to the 1.6.0/1.7.0 update-options work). ShipLog now triggers the update
  the proven way again  -  by clicking Unraid's own "apply update" control on the row  -  and
  only automates its dialogs on top: it auto-confirms Unraid's prompt when "Ask before
  updating" is off, and hides the log window when "Silent update" is on. If those dialogs
  ever look different on your build, the update still runs  -  it just shows normally.
- FIX: "Silent update" (and one-click update with "Ask before updating" off) did nothing  - 
  the container still showed an update after a refresh. ShipLog now fires Unraid's own update
  through Unraid's own call, so its CSRF token rides along and the update actually runs; in
  silent mode only the log window is hidden, and the row clears when the update finishes.
- FIX: digest-pinned containers (image@sha256:..., also with a tag) were stuck on a
  permanent "could not resolve upstream" error or showed un-actionable updates; they
  now show an honest neutral "pinned" state with zero registry cost.
- FIX: locally built images could match a FOREIGN Docker Hub repository of the same
  name and show a wrong update with a foreign changelog; they are now recognised and
  labelled "local image" (also covers docker-load'ed images; re-pull once to re-attach
  a side-loaded image to its registry).
- FIX: images pulled through a mirror no longer show a phantom "digest drift".
- FIX: the "Update all" button now really sits directly LEFT of the Basic/Advanced
  view toggle. Unraid's toggle row is a right-packing flex container, so the button
  is placed inside it as the first element; before this it flowed to the far left
  edge of the page.
- Critical uninstall fix (the same bug found and fixed in TowerTorch). The
  removal script was wrapped in a bare `&lt;REMOVE&gt;` tag, which Unraid's plugin
  manager does NOT execute (it only runs `&lt;FILE Method="remove"&gt;` blocks) - so the
  cleanup silently never ran: the engine daemon kept running and the plugin files,
  the installed package and the cached flash .txz all stayed behind after
  "uninstall" (Unraid still moved the flash .plg off, because a remove that runs
  nothing trivially "succeeds"). Removal is now a proper
  `&lt;FILE Run="/bin/bash" Method="remove"&gt;` block ending in `exit 0` (Unraid only
  clears the flash .plg on a zero exit, else it reinstalls on boot); it stops the
  daemon by PID, removes the exact registered package, wipes the dir + cached .txz,
  and keeps your appdata + settings. Installs now also wipe old plugin files first
  so nothing orphans across updates.
- The settings Apply button no longer stays greyed out after Ollama Autodetect
  (a programmatically filled field now announces its change to Unraid).
- Survive a reboot. The engine no longer starts before the array/pools are
  mounted: rc.shiplog defers until DATA_DIR is on a real filesystem (not the root
  tmpfs), so the SQLite version-memory persists across restarts instead of being
  wiped every boot, and it no longer risks blocking the user share from mounting.
  event/started brings the daemon up once the array is online.

🌐 **Translations**
- All new settings text is translated across the 26 supported languages.

### 1.0.0
🎨 **Design**
- Card settings page; the bubble follows Unraid's light/dark theme and language.

⚡ **Improved**
- A changelog control next to every container in Unraid's native Docker tab: the
  version jump, a deterministic risk badge, the release notes, and a DEPRECATED
  badge when the upstream repo is archived. Up-to-date containers show the notes
  of the version they're running.
- Optional AI summaries via a local Ollama and Matrix notifications on new
  updates  -  each with a live "Check" button in the settings.
- Read-only by construction (read-only Docker socket); the engine runs as a host
  daemon, no separate container.
</CHANGES>

<!-- Pre-install: installpkg never deletes files a newer version dropped, so the
     plugin dir is wiped first. Appdata and the .cfg on the flash stay, and so does
     this version's package: Unraid installs every .plg again at boot, often before
     the network is up. -->
<FILE Run="/bin/bash">
<INLINE>
&emhttp;/scripts/rc.shiplog stop 2>/dev/null
rm -rf &emhttp;
find &plgPath; -maxdepth 1 -name '&slug;-*.txz' ! -name '&txz;' -delete 2>/dev/null || true
</INLINE>
</FILE>

<!--
  plugin/pkg_build.sh builds the package for the GitHub release "v&version;";
  the release workflow fills in the SHA256.
-->
<FILE Name="&plgPath;/&txz;" Run="/sbin/upgradepkg --install-new --reinstall">
  <URL>&pkgURL;/&txz;</URL>
  <SHA256>0de3d6df01600b817745b4cd7df76253de7543c4a487ee6793ae38aab7deb846</SHA256>
</FILE>

<!-- Post-install: (re)start the engine daemon immediately, no reboot needed. -->
<FILE Run="/bin/bash">
<INLINE>
chmod +x &emhttp;/bin/shiplog &emhttp;/scripts/rc.shiplog &emhttp;/event/* 2>/dev/null
# The cfg holds tokens, and update.php creates it readable by everyone.
if [ -f &plgPath;/&slug;.cfg ]; then chmod 600 &plgPath;/&slug;.cfg; fi
&emhttp;/scripts/rc.shiplog restart
echo ""
cat &emhttp;/banner.txt 2>/dev/null
echo ""
echo "ShipLog ist ready!"
</INLINE>
</FILE>

<!-- Unraid's plugin manager runs only <FILE> elements and ignores a bare <REMOVE>,
     so removal is a <FILE Method="remove"> block. It has to exit 0, or Unraid
     keeps the .plg on the flash and reinstalls it on boot. 'pkill -f &slug;'
     would also kill this script and its `plugin remove` parent, so a stray
     daemon is matched by its full binary path. The flash .cfg stays. -->
<FILE Run="/bin/bash" Method="remove">
<INLINE>
# The banner has to print while its file still exists.
cat &emhttp;/banner.txt 2>/dev/null
echo ""
echo "Bye!"
echo ""
&emhttp;/scripts/rc.shiplog stop 2>/dev/null
for p in $(pgrep -f '&emhttp;/bin/&slug;' 2>/dev/null); do kill -9 "$p" 2>/dev/null; done
pkg=$(ls /var/log/packages/ 2>/dev/null | grep -E "^&slug;(-[^-]+-[^-]+-[^-]+)?$" | head -n1)
if [ -n "$pkg" ]; then removepkg "$pkg" 2>/dev/null; fi
rm -rf &emhttp;
rm -f &plgPath;/*.txz &plgPath;/*.txz.md5 /var/run/&slug;.pid /var/run/&slug;.sock
# The OpenRouter key goes with the plugin. A key left behind is one nobody remembers to revoke.
rm -f &plgPath;/openrouter.key
# The appdata with the version history stays: a read-only advisor should not
# delete a folder the user can see, and a reinstall resumes with the history.
echo "ShipLog removed, and with it the OpenRouter key. Settings (flash .cfg) and the version history at"
echo "/mnt/user/appdata/&slug; were kept; delete that folder manually if you"
echo "don't plan to reinstall."
exit 0
</INLINE>
</FILE>

</PLUGIN>
